A||||8||88formula|| Engine has serious driveability symptoms. Weird!||||1||
Z||000000||88formula||09-25-2001||10:39 PM||||Really screwed up running engine.<P>My engine runs weird. I have not had much time to diagnose it and I don’t have a scan tool. I did do a run through with the map and tps sensor and they are fine. The O2 sensor is new and the coolant temp sensor is new. The engine is new and just about everything else is new. I have a 3.4-liter engine. My base timing is set at 12 degrees before TDC and I’m running the ohv 3.4 injectors with the engine. The fuel pressure is 40.5 psi. The IAC valve has been cleaned and is working correctly. I checked all my grounds using the factory service manual. I checked for vacuum leaks with propane enrichment and found none. There appears to be nothing wrong with the engine. I did get a code 13 but when I check the 02 sensor ground it checks out fine. I have no cat. <P>The idle lopes up and down from 800 to 1500 rpm with the engine in closed loop. The engine hesitates and surges at part throttle and is gutless under 4000 rpm compared to what it was when I first ran it. As soon as I hit 4000 rpm at any throttle position its like someone hit the nitrous switch and the engine has full power. If I hold the throttle under no load at about 3000 rpm (about 8% throttle) the engine rpm will dip down to 1200 rpm and then rebound back up to the 3k rpm while I’m trying to maintain 3k rpm. It does this with MY FOOT STEADY ON THE GAS under no load. While I’m cruising at any rpm from about 30mph to 50 mph the engine almost cuts out and then surges and keeps doing this making the car slow down and then surge forward when I push the pedal a little more.<P>When I put a timing light on and point the timing light at the damper to check my timing the timing mark jumps around erratically indicating that there may be something wrong with the ignition. The timing chain is a new Cloyes double roller timing chain so it’s not a loose chain. The engine skips at idle and runs crappy. The engine also runs rich indicated by the black exhaust tips with only 1500 miles on the engine.    <P>If I unplug the O2 sensor thereby forcing the computer to stay in open loop the engine runs great and exhibits none of these symptoms.  The idle however is high at around 1100 rpm fully warmed up. I think I may have a cracked EGR tube or some other vacuum leak but I would like to here your thoughts before I tackle it this weekend. The idle dips down and them surges as the 02 sensor swings in voltage from lean too rich. The weirdest part is how the engine all of a sudden wakes up after 4k rpm at any throttle position.  3800 rpm and 4000 rpm are like night and day! <P>Voltage is 13 volts with engine running. I have a 5-speed manual transmission.   <P><BR>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG">  <p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 09-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||c m west||09-25-2001||10:47 PM||||well - ill stick around to help you fix this - is your distributor shaft sloppy from side to side - also, try setting your timing at 8 degrees<P>Eric - West Coast Fiero <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com" TARGET=_blank>www.cwestco.com</A> <BR>310-305-4111<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mach10||09-26-2001||01:04 AM||||floppy timing says that SOMETHiNG is loose. loose dist/shaft, or loose chain? Can't remember the structure, but isn't there a tensioner?<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000003||87GTZ34||09-26-2001||09:46 AM||||88 you failed to mention if your computer is still calibrated for the 2.8 air flow characteristics.  If you haven't remapped then read below otherwise please disregard. The fact that open loop seems to make it run more consistent (not necessarily correct though) would lead me to believe that the closed loop tables don't match your hardware.  Also,  knowing what I know about engines, the fuel maps are different for your old & new engines.  If you are so inclined, borrow a scanner and get the injector pulse times to calculate the fuel flow for a specific RPM and see if it comes close to that specific air that should be flowing.  <BR>I don't think you have an ignition problem as I had a 'jumpy' ignition timing that I've already corrected.  I had a very sloppy timing chain but replacing it only improved the jumpiness ever so slightly.  I had bad injectors.  What was happening was that a misfire (even a slight one) would not allow the air to flow 'normally' for exhaust and hence didn't pull in the same amount of air and messed up the vacuum.  The computer read this and would compensate with timing to keep the engine running.  I wouldn't say you need injectors w/o knowing anything about them, but I would say the timing issue is a symptom and not a cause.  Hook up a vac gauge to manifold vacuum and watch it dance.<BR>If you decide to try to recalibrate you will most likely need to have someone that understands fuel maps for modified engines burn one for you.  You have a modified 3.4 with a restrictive 2.8 intake and running the 3.4 injectors.  Fuel flow is fairly predictable given the specs for the injectors but your engine combination has not been thoroughly characterized so you will most likely need to use a trial & error method especially if you want to run it hard or race it.  You're probably aware of this but it is much more difficult to engineer (anything) across a broad range of factors vs. and narrow range.  GL||||reg||1
Z||000004||Black88GT||09-26-2001||12:31 PM||||What size injectors are you using?  Do you have the 3.4 chip?  If not then...  <P>My advice, get the chip for no other reason but ditching the EGR.  It's nothing but problems.<P>Also an MSD ignition box made my 3.4 run much much better.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88formula||09-26-2001||05:09 PM||||C m west I checked my distributor during my engine rebuild and it is tight. The car only has 50k miles on it. I used to have my timing set at 16 degrees BTDC because it improved my idle and I had no detonation. The weird closed loop phenomena however got worse so backed it down to 12 degrees and this improved the situation somewhat. I'll try 8-degrees and see what happens. <P>Mach10 I agree with you but my timing chain is new and tight and my distributor is tight. The Cloyes double roller timing chain cannot be used with a tensioner according to its instructions. My idle however is unstable and the engine misfires at idle. <P>87GTZ34 With the original 2.8 prom the condition was worse and the engine ran too rich. I had a friend send me a prom that he used on his 3.4 that leans out the fuel map a little for closed loop operation. It helped the rich mixture but the problem still remains. To answer your question yes I basically still have a near stock prom chip and fuel map. What gets me is people in my fiero club with stock 3.4 engines have no problems with their 3.4-liter engines and they have the stock prom and 3.4 injectors. <P>My injectors have about 75k miles on then and I suspect they may be a little leaky or clogged making the symptoms exaggerated. My engine vacuum is a stable 19 inches at idle, actually it goes from 18-19 inches as the idle fluctuates. At a higher rpm (2k and above) it climbs to about 22 inches of vacuum. I've checked the engine mechanical condition and mechanically it’s very strong. <P>Black88GT I have the stock ohv 3.4 injectors that came with the Camaro engine. I think they flow 17 lbs. an hour or so I've been told. If I wanted to ditch the EGR than I would just unplug it. The EGR plays an important function with emissions the octane resistance and messing around with it is not what I want to do. I've seen what detonation can do to a piston and I don't want it to happen to mine. It is possible to just enrichen the fuel mixture and then you wouldn't need the EGR but that would give me cylinder wash, bad gas mileage, and a shorter engine life. Besides the stock computer disables the ERG at full throttle anyway! <P>Here is what I have for modifications to help the engine breath better,<P>Heads<BR>Intake ports have been very mildly ported and cleaned up<BR>Exhaust ports have been polished<BR>Three angle valve job on intake seat and radius on the exhaust valve seat<BR>Valves have been backcut 20-degrees for more low lift flow<BR>SI stainless steel valves swirl polished.<BR>GM 110 lb. Valve springs and matching retainers set to correct installed height.<BR> <BR>Exhaust<BR>FOCOA headers<BR>Borla exhaust<BR>No cat<P>Intake<BR>Stock gasket matched manifolds<BR>Polished throttle body<BR>Stock air cleaner with new filter<P>Fuel <BR>Stock fuel pump<BR>3.4 17 lb. An hour injectors with 75k miles<BR>Stock 2.8 prom chip with leaned out closed loop fuel map<P>Engine<BR>9.05 compression ratio<BR>GM 12363220 camshaft with 204 degrees of intake and 216 degrees of exhaust duration measured at .050”. Lift is .427/. 454-lobe separation angle is 112 degrees<BR>The stock 3.4 camshaft has 195 degrees intake and 214 degrees exhaust duration measured at .050”. Lift is .390/. 405 and the lobe separation angle is 106<P>The camshaft I have now is the same camshaft that comes with the GM crate 3.4-liter engine and I think it is very mild. <P> <P><BR><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/88formula_logo_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||87GTZ34||09-26-2001||05:24 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88formula:<BR>The engine hesitates and surges at part throttle and is gutless under 4000 rpm compared to what it was when I first ran it. As soon as I hit 4000 rpm at any throttle position its like someone hit the nitrous switch and the engine has full power<BR>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>This symptom sounds like my 2.8 b4 I changed the injectors except it was about 2800 rpm and of course no nitrous.  If you search here there are others with the 'hunting idle' problem that have done 3.4 swaps.  One commonality seems to be that most have a free flowing exhaust but that could be just coincidence.  It is not that expensive to have you injectors reverse flow cleaned and characterized, but I don't think will solve all of your engine problems althought it should run better.  I had Marren do a set for me b4. GL<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||theogre||09-26-2001||07:29 PM||||check the charging system carefully with good test equipment. check the thin positive lead from the battery, the large terminal below C500, and the heavy lead between the alternator and the terminal below C500. <P>GM has released TSBs over the years about checking the charging system anytime you've got an unstable idle and can't find any obvious cause.<P>C500 is the big block next to the battery. You want the single large terminal under it.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||09-27-2001||03:32 PM||||OK Thanks guys I'll check those things Sunday. ||||reg||1
